  "id": "villanella_napoletana",
  "name": "Villanella alla napoletana (Neapolitan villanesca)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Light three-voice part-song in Neapolitan dialect that appeared in print in Venice from 1537 as the canzone villanesca alla napolitana, with Giovan Domenico da Nola among its first named composers, and was soon taken up and refined by northern composers including Adrian Willaert and Orlande de Lassus. Its strophic, mostly homophonic settings of rustic and comic love poems deliberately keep the parallel fifths of popular singing and parody the refined madrigal.",
